    "body": "Peninsula Energy says a Nov. 20 cyberattack hit the operating server at its Lance uranium mine in Wyoming. Credit: AdobeStock by terovesalainen\nPosted By: Henry Lazenby November 23, 2026\nPeninsula Energy (ASX: PEN; US-OTC: PENMF) warned Monday that a cyberattack would temporarily disrupt its Lance uranium mine in Wyoming, where low flows from production wells had already forced the company to abandon this year’s output target.\nThe breach occurred Friday at Lance, about 380 km north of state capital Cheyenne. Peninsula put its recovery plan into effect and asked the Australian Securities Exchange to halt trading. It hasn’t said whether uranium production stopped or how long the disruption might last.\n“The cyberattack was on the Lance project’s operating server and system,” CEO George Bauk told shareholders at Monday’s annual meeting. He said the administrative systems at the mine and the corporate office were unaffected.\nAngloGold Ashanti (NYSE: AU) kept its mines running after it detected unauthorized activity on some of its systems on Oct. 16. The gold miner said in its Nov. 5 quarterly report that it believed it had contained the breach. Industrial cybersecurity firm Dragos tracked 19 ransomware incidents involving mining companies worldwide from July through September. Neither miner has identified the type of attack it faced.\nSuppliers hit\nCyberattacks have also hit companies that supply miners. Australian mining software provider Scope Systems offered customers help doing work manually after a May 5 attack encrypted servers it hosted for them. It took until May 17 to restore them all from backups, the company said.\nNo public evidence links either mining incident to this month’s crippling events in the U.S. involving the FBI’s crime database, Microsoft Outlook email services, the Agriculture Department’s farm loan software, as well as aviation and banking services.\nLance restart\nLance recovers uranium by in-situ recovery, pumping a solution through underground ore and bringing the dissolved metal to the surface. Slow flow rates prompted Peninsula in July to withdraw its 2026 target of 400,000–500,000 lb. of uranium oxide. Before the breach, it was aiming for 500,000–600,000 lb. in 2027.\nWorkers packed 15,588 lb. into drums in the quarter to Sept. 30, up from 11,482 lb. in the June quarter. Peninsula made no uranium sales in the September quarter. Bauk said Monday the company had about 40,000 lb. in inventory and was waiting for Cameco (TSX: CCO; NYSE: CCJ) to sign off on product specifications before trucking it off site.\nPeninsula shares last traded at A16¢ on Friday, valuing the company at A$91.4 million (C$87 million). The trading halt runs until Peninsula issues an update or trading opens Wednesday, whichever comes first.\nPrint\nCOMMODITY: Uranium\nREGION: Australia, NZ & South Pacific United States\nCOMPANY: AngloGold Ashanti Peninsula Energy\nRepublish this article",
